Here’s one woven roadmap. It assumes roughly 15–20 hours a week, which gets you job-ready in about 9–12 months. Faster is possible with more hours, but the real bottleneck is building projects, not watching lectures.

Principle that keeps it short: don’t finish one field before starting the next. From Phase 1 onward you’ll build one project that grows through every phase: data → model → deployed service → monitored → LLM-powered.

Phase 0: Python and tooling (weeks 1–4)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Python: CS50P (Harvard, free) or Kaggle’s “Python” and “Pandas” micro-courses. Then Automate the Boring Stuff (free online) for practical fluency.
Tooling you’ll use forever: Git/GitHub, the terminal, virtual environments (uv or venv), Jupyter, VS Code.
SQL: Mode’s SQL tutorial or SQLBolt. Data scientists use SQL daily.
Math, just enough: 3Blue1Brown’s Essence of Linear Algebra and Essence of Calculus, plus StatQuest (Josh Starmer) for statistics. Don’t study math in isolation; revisit it when a concept confuses you.


Phase 1: Data science foundations (weeks 5–10)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NumPy, pandas, Matplotlib/Seaborn via Kaggle Learn (Data Cleaning, Data Visualization, Feature Engineering).
Statistics for DS: StatQuest playlists, then Think Stats (Allen Downey, free PDF).
Practice: do 2–3 Kaggle datasets end to end (EDA, cleaning, a written insight summary). Publish notebooks on GitHub.

Phase 2: Classical machine learning (weeks 11–18)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Core course: Andrew Ng’s Machine Learning Specialization (Coursera audit is free) or fast.ai’s Practical Deep Learning if you prefer top-down learning.
Hands-on: scikit-learn’s official docs and tutorials; Kaggle’s “Intro/Intermediate ML.”
Topics: regression, trees, gradient boosting (XGBoost/LightGBM), cross-validation, metrics, overfitting, feature engineering.
Practice: enter a Kaggle “Getting Started” competition. Your goal is a clean, reproducible pipeline, not a leaderboard rank.

Phase 3: Deep learning and transformers (weeks 19–28)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Andrej Karpathy’s Neural Networks: Zero to Hero (YouTube). It’s among the best free resources for understanding how neural nets, backprop, and GPT work from scratch.
PyTorch: official tutorials, plus fast.ai Part 1 and Part 2.
Transformers: Karpathy’s “Let’s build GPT,” Jay Alammar’s The Illustrated Transformer, and the Hugging Face LLM Course (free).
Practice: fine-tune a small pretrained model (e.g., text classification) with Hugging Face.

Phase 4: MLOps (weeks 29–38)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Core course: DataTalks.Club’s MLOps Zoomcamp (free, project-based) and Made With ML (Goku Mohandas, free).
Skills: experiment tracking (MLflow or Weights & Biases), data/model versioning (DVC), Docker, FastAPI for serving, CI/CD with GitHub Actions, workflow orchestration (Prefect or Airflow), cloud basics (AWS/GCP free tiers), monitoring and drift detection (Evidently).
Practice: take your Phase 2 model and deploy it as a containerized API with automated tests, tracked experiments, and a retraining pipeline.

Phase 5: LLMs, RAG and agents (weeks 39–48)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Courses: DeepLearning.AI short courses (free), covering prompt engineering, RAG, agents, evaluation, and fine-tuning. Also the Hugging Face LLM and Agents courses, and DataTalks.Club’s LLM Zoomcamp (RAG-focused).
Skills:
RAG: embeddings, chunking, vector databases (Chroma, FAISS, pgvector), hybrid search, reranking.
Evaluation: building eval sets, LLM-as-judge, retrieval metrics. Many teams skip this, and it’s what separates professionals from hobbyists.
Fine-tuning: LoRA/QLoRA via Hugging Face PEFT.
Agents and tool use: function calling, orchestration frameworks (LangChain/LlamaIndex, but learn the raw API first).
LLMOps: tracing, cost and latency monitoring, guardrails, serving (vLLM).
Reading: the Anthropic and OpenAI docs and cookbooks (free), plus Chip Huyen’s writing on AI engineering.
Practice: build a RAG app over a real document set, with an eval suite, deployed using your Phase 4 pipeline.

Phase 6: Proof of expertise (ongoing, overlapping weeks 30+)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Portfolio: 3–4 polished GitHub projects with READMEs: one classical ML, one deep learning, one end-to-end MLOps, one RAG/LLM system.
Read papers: start with “Attention Is All You Need,” then follow Papers With Code and Sebastian Raschka’s newsletter.
Community and signal: Kaggle, write short posts about what you build, and contribute to an open-source library.
Paid optional: a certification like AWS ML Specialty or Google’s Professional ML Engineer is the only place where spending money may help, mainly for hiring filters.

How to compress the timeline
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Project-first: watch a lecture, then immediately apply it. Passive watching feels productive but isn’t.
Skip what you don’t need yet: you can start Phase 5 early if LLMs are your priority, since the API-level work needs only Python and Phase 1 basics. Then loop back for depth.
Timebox everything: if a course stalls you for more than two weeks, move on.
Use AI as a tutor: paste confusing code or concepts into Claude and ask for explanations and review of your projects.
